{"i":3,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"id\": \"contract.json\", \"pointer\": \"/source_steps\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": [{\"id\": \"S001\", \"text\": \"Fix X,A,a and assume L1: a>0. Work with X's Borel structure, so M001 applies and gives H^a(A)=sup_{r>0} content(a,r,A) over extended nonnegative r. For arbitrary δ, temporarily assume L2: δ>0. At the finite scale ofReal δ, every admissible covering member has finite extended diameter, hence is bounded and has ediam equal to its real diameter cast to ℝ≥0∞. The nonemptiness-proof supremum in D004 is the displayed diameter power on a nonempty member and is zero on an empty member. For a>0 this agrees with assigning the empty member diameter zero and 0^a=0. Thus the nested infima are exactly the original admissible-cover infimum, with no prefactor. A finite family can be padded by empty sets. For an arbitrary countable index type choose an injection into ℕ; at each image index put the unique original member and elsewhere put ∅. This preserves the union and all nonnegative sums. Conversely an ℕ-family is countable. Discharge L2 after this finite-scale interpretation, retaining its δ>0 antecedent for every δ. For arbitrary δ₁,δ₂, temporarily assume L3: 0<δ₁≤δ₂. Every δ₁-cover is a δ₂-cover, so C^a_δ₂(A)≤C^a_δ₁(A). Discharge L3 and quantify the two scales. Every cost bounds its infimum above; a lower bound on every admissible cost bounds the infimum below. If the admissible family is empty its infimum is ∞, and the latter assertion remains true. For any positive extended scale r, if it is finite it is the cast of a positive real scale. The content at r=∞ is at most that at any fixed finite positive scale, since every finite-scale cover is also an ∞-cover. Hence the ∞ scale adds nothing to the supremum. We have proved H^a(A)=sup_{δ>0}C^a_δ(A), and each such content is ≤H^a(A).\"}, {\"id\": \"S002\", \"text\": \"For the arbitrary δ,ε, temporarily assume L4: δ>0 and ε>0. Suppose L5: C^a_δ(A)=0. if every admissible cost were ≥ε, the lower-bound property in F001 would imply C^a_δ(A)≥ε>0, a contradiction. Thus an admissible cover of cost <ε exists. Discharge L5 to obtain the forward implication. In terms of the nested infima in D004, any failed cover or diameter condition has value ∞ and cannot give a value <ε, so the approximation provides an actual admissible family. Conversely assume L6: for every positive tolerance there exists an admissible cover of smaller cost. This forces the nonnegative content to be zero: a positive value, finite or infinite, has some positive finite real below it, contradicting the upper bound by those costs. Discharge L6, then L4, and quantify the arbitrary positive scale and tolerance. This proves the zero-content approximation equivalence for every positive scale. These arguments also cover an empty admissible family, whose content is ∞.\"}, {\"id\": \"S003\", \"text\": \"By F001, H^a(A)=0 implies each positive-scale content is zero; F002 then gives the required arbitrarily small covers at every scale. Conversely, those covers make every positive-scale content zero by F002, and their supremum is zero by F001. This proves the full equivalence and all asserted content properties. Discharge L1 to retain a>0 as an antecedent and universally quantify X,A,a and the scale/tolerance variables. No boundedness or measurability assumption on A is used.\"}, {\"id\": \"S004\", \"text\": \"Fix X,A,q an…30 tokens truncated… r≤δ. By N001 with exponent q, scale r and tolerance ε/4 there exists a countable r-cover U of A whose total q-cost is <ε/4; reindex it as a sequence with empty padding if necessary. In particular its members have finite diameter.\"}, {\"id\": \"S005\", \"text\": \"Choose U from F001 and put d_i=diam(U_i), with d_i=0 on empty members. For each natural i define a_seq(i)=min(r,(ε·2^(-i-3))^(1/q)). Both entries of this minimum are positive, so 0<a_seq(i)≤r and a_seq(i)^q≤ε·2^(-i-3). The nonnegative geometric series has sum Σ_{i≥0}2^(-i-3)=1/4 (its partial sums are (1/4)(1-2^(-m)) for the first m terms). Hence Σ a_seq(i)^q≤ε/4. Put r_i=max(d_i,a_seq(i)). Then 0<r_i≤r≤1 and r_i^q=max(d_i^q,a_seq(i)^q)≤d_i^q+a_seq(i)^q. Summing nonnegative terms, using F001 and the geometric bound, gives Σ r_i^q<ε/2. Numerical padding changes no covering set and works even if X is a singleton.\"}, {\"id\": \"S006\", \"text\": \"For each natural i put N_i=ceil(1/r_i), a positive integer. Then 1/N_i≤r_i and N_i≤1/r_i+1≤2/r_i, since 0<r_i≤1. The closed intervals [h/N_i,(h+1)/N_i], 0≤h<N_i, cover I. Define W_seq(i,h)=U_i×[h/N_i,(h+1)/N_i]. In the maximum product metric any two points of this product have distance at most r_i, since the first-coordinate diameter is d_i≤r_i and the second-coordinate interval length is 1/N_i≤r_i. The product family covers A×I, and its index set {(i,h):i∈ℕ,0≤h<N_i} is countable. Empty products have zero cost. By N001 this countable family may be reindexed into ℕ without changing the union or sum. It is a δ-cover, and its total (q+1)-cost is at most Σ_i N_i r_i^(q+1)≤2Σ_i r_i^q<ε. The power cancellation r_i^(q+1)/r_i=r_i^q is valid because r_i>0. Rearranging these nonnegative sums is legitimate, and the upper bound is finite.\"}, {\"id\": \"S007\", \"text\": \"F003 holds for arbitrary positive δ,ε. Discharge L2 to obtain existence of the stated arbitrarily fine arbitrarily cheap covers. Since q+1>0, N001, now for MaxProd(X), A×I and exponent q+1, implies H^(q+1)(A×I)=0. Discharge L1 and universally quantify X,A,q. This proves the output proposition on the whole asserted domain without any positive-diameter assumption on the covering sets.\"}, {\"id\": \"S008\", \"text\": \"Fix X,A,s,δ and assume L1: s>0 and δ>0. Set c=C^s_δ(A), possibly ∞. Take any sequence V and assume L2: V is a δ-cover of A×I in MaxProd(X). Put ρ_i=diam(V_i), A_i=π_X(V_i), B_i=π_ℝ(V_i). Every V_i is bounded, since its extended diameter is ≤δ<∞. Each projection has pairwise distances at most ρ_i, whence diam A_i≤ρ_i and diam B_i≤ρ_i≤δ. For a nonempty V_i, B_i is nonempty and bounded above and below: choose any one of its points and use the finite diameter bound. Thus inf B_i and sup B_i exist as real numbers. Put J_i=[inf B_i,sup B_i]. The least-upper-bound and greatest-lower-bound properties give B_i⊆J_i and sup B_i-inf B_i≤ρ_i: for every b,b'∈B_i one has b-b'≤ρ_i, and taking supremum in b then infimum in b' preserves the inequality. In particular inf B_i≤sup B_i. For empty V_i put ρ_i=0 and A_i=B_i=J_i=∅. Each J_i is a closed real interval or ∅, hence Borel measurable, and all projection bounds remain valid. This construction imposes no measurability on V_i or its projections.\"}, {\"id\": \"S009\", \"text\": \"For each nonempty member, apply M009 with endpoints inf B_i and sup B_i. By F001, volume(J_i)=ofReal(sup B_i-inf B_i)≤ρ_i. For an empty member volume(J_i)=volume(∅)=0=ρ_i by the empty-set axiom of a measure. Also I=[0,1] is closed and measurable and M009 with endpoints 0,1 gives volume(I)=1. Since I∩J_i⊆J_i, measure monotonicity gives volume(I∩J_i)≤volume(J_i)≤ρ_i; each intersection is measurable as an intersection of closed measurable sets.\"}, {\"id\": \"S010\", \"text\": \"Define g(t)=1_I(t)c and f_seq(i)(t)=1_{I∩J_i}(t)ρ_i^s. Fix any real t. In the case L3: t∈I, the family consisting of A_i when t∈J_i and ∅ otherwise covers A: under the auxiliary antecedent L4: x∈A, L2 supplies an i with (x,t)∈V_i, so x∈A_i and t∈B_i⊆J_i. This proves membership in the projected union; discharge L4 and quantify x. Each such projected set has diameter ≤ρ_i≤δ by F001. N001 therefore gives c≤Σ_i ρ_i^s 1_{J_i}(t)=Σ_i f_seq(i)(t). Discharge L3 after the inside-I argument. If t∉I, g(t)=0 and every f_seq(i)(t)=0 by the indicator zero branch, so the same inequality holds. Universal introduction in t gives g≤Σ_i f_seq(i) pointwise on ℝ, also if c=∞. F002 shows that every indicator summand is Borel measurable: its only values are a nonnegative constant and zero on complementary measurable pieces, hence it is also volume-almost-everywhere measurable. No product of an undefined infinite real quantity is used.\"}, {\"id\": \"S011\", \"text\": \"Integrate F003 over ℝ with Lebesgue measure. M011 gives lintegral(volume,g)≤lintegral(volume,Σ_i f_seq(i)). M012 for I and constant c, together with volume(I)=1 from F002, gives lintegral(volume,g)=c·1=c, even when c=∞. M010 for the countable family f_seq exchanges the nonnegative series and lower integral, since F003 established almost-everywhere measurability of each summand. M012 for I∩J_i and constant ρ_i^s evaluates each term. Thus\\nc=c·volume(I)\\n ≤Σ_i ρ_i^s volume(I∩J_i)\\n ≤Σ_i ρ_i^s volume(J_i)\\n ≤Σ_i ρ_i^s ρ_i\\n =Σ_i ρ_i^(s+1).\\nThe middle bounds use F002 and monotonicity of multiplication and nonnegative sums. Each ρ_i is finite and nonnegative, and ρ_i^s·ρ_i=ρ_i^(s+1) holds for positive ρ_i by the real-power law and for ρ_i=0 because s>0. This is an ℝ≥0∞ computation; neither c nor the series need be finite. Discharge L2: every admissible δ-cover has (s+1)-cost at least c.\"}, {\"id\": \"S012\", \"text\": \"By the lower-infimum bound in N001, F004 implies C^s_δ(A)≤C^(s+1)_δ(A×I), including if no admissible product cover exists. This holds at every δ>0. Taking suprema over positive scales and using N001 for both positive exponents s and s+1 gives H^s(A)≤H^(s+1)(A×I). Discharge L1 and universally quantify X,A,s and the scale. These are the complete scale and measure inequalities asserted in the output proposition.\"}, {\"id\": \"S013\", \"text\": \"Define e_h=e_basis(h) by the standard coordinate formula: its h-th coordinate is 1 and every other coordinate is 0. The n₀ coordinate unit vectors form a real basis of Rn(n₀): every vector is its finite coordinate linear combination, and equality of coordinates forces all coefficients in a zero combination to vanish. There are n₀ vectors. For n₀=0 this is the empty basis of the zero vector space. In MaxProd(Rn(n₀)), the n₀ vectors (e_h,0) together with (0,1) similarly span uniquely, by the first coordinates and last coordinate, so its real dimension is n₀+1. Both are real normed vector spaces (the second with the maximum product norm) and finite dimensional. Apply M007 to their full spaces and M006 to E⊆univ Rn(n₀) and F⊆univ MaxProd(Rn(n₀)). We obtain 0≤D≤n₀ and 0≤T≤n₀+1. The lower zero bounds follow from the ℝ≥0∞ codomain of dimH. Thus D and T are finite, including when n₀=0. A finite extended nonnegative real is the cast of its unique nonnegative real representative, and casts preserve finite sums and order.\"}, {\"id\": \"S014\", \"text\": \"The real line has basis {1}, hence real dimension one. The set I=[0,1] contains the open interval (0,1) in its interior: for each of its points the distance to the two endpoints has a positive minimum, giving a contained open ball. In particular 1/2 belongs to the interior. Apply M013 to the real normed space ℝ and A=I. Its hypotheses hold and it gives dimH(I)=1.\"}, {\"id\": \"S015\", \"text\": \"H001 supplies x_f∈E. Define f_f(t)=(x_f,t). For any real t,u, the maximum product distance between f_f(t) and f_f(u) is max(dist(x_f,x_f),|t-u|)=|t-u|. Thus f_f is an isometry; its image of I is {x_f}×I, a subset of F by x_f∈E.\"}, {\"id\": \"S016\", \"text\": \"Apply M008 to f_f and I, using the isometry in F003. It gives dimH(f_f '' I)=dimH(I)=1 by F002. Apply M006 to f_f '' I⊆F, also in F003, to get 1≤dimH(F)=T. Combining with F001 proves 0≤D≤n₀<∞ and 1≤T≤n₀+1<∞, together with dimH(I)=1 and the finite real identifications. The chosen point and its map do not occur in the output proposition.\"}, {\"id\": \"S017\", \"text\": \"By N004, write D and T as casts of finite real values d_real≥0 and k_real≥1, respectively. Order and addition of these finite values agree with their real operations.\"}, {\"id\": \"S018\", \"text\": \"Fix arbitrary real q and assume L1: q>d_real. F001 gives q>0, so q defines a nonnegative real exponent. M003 with set E and exponent q gives H^q(E)=0, since dimH(E)=D<q. Apply N002 to X=Rn(n₀), A=E and q. Its positive-exponent and zero-measure premises have just been established, and E×I in MaxProd(Rn(n₀)) is F. Therefore H^(q+1)(F)=0.\"}, {\"id\": \"S019\", \"text\": \"Since q+1>0, it is also a nonnegative real exponent. By F002 its Hausdorff measure on F is zero, hence different from ∞. Apply M004 with F and exponent q+1. It gives T≤q+1, equivalently k_real≤q+1 by F001. Discharge L1: for every real q>d_real one has k_real≤q+1.\"}, {\"id\": \"S020\", \"text\": \"Assume for contradiction L2: k_real>d_real+1. Put q_mid=(d_real+k_real-1)/2. Then d_real<q_mid<k_real-1, because k_real-1>d_real. F003 with q=q_mid gives k_real≤q_mid+1<k_real, a contradiction. Discharge L2 to obtain k_real≤d_real+1. Cast back by F001: T≤D+1. Finiteness is already supplied by F001. This proves the output proposition for every natural n₀, including zero.\"}, {\"id\": \"S021\", \"text\": \"By N004 write D,T as casts of real values d_real≥0 and k_real≥1. Their orders and addition agree with real operations. If L3: d_real=0, the fibre bound k_real≥1 gives k_real≥d_real+1. Discharge L3 to retain this implication. Since d_real≥0, the other case is d_real>0.\"}, {\"id\": \"S022\", \"text\": \"Assume L1: d_real>0. To prove the lower bound, assume for contradiction L2: k_real<d_real+1. Then 0≤k_real-1<d_real by F001. Put s_mid=(k_real-1+d_real)/2. These inequalities imply 0<s_mid<d_real and k_real<s_mid+1. The strict positivity of s_mid follows from d_real>0 even if k_real-1=0.\"}, {\"id\": \"S023\", \"text\": \"By F002, M002 applied to E at the nonnegative exponent s_mid gives H^s_mid(E)=∞, because dimH(E)=D is the cast of d_real. N003, applied with X=Rn(n₀), A=E and s=s_mid, gives H^s_mid(E)≤H^(s_mid+1)(F). Consequently H^(s_mid+1)(F)=∞.\"}, {\"id\": \"S024\", \"text\": \"M005 with A=F and the nonnegative exponent s_mid+1 yields s_mid+1≤T from F003, equivalently s_mid+1≤k_real by F001. This contradicts F002. Discharge L2 and conclude k_real≥d_real+1 under L1. Discharge L1, and combine with the d_real=0 implication in F001 by exhaustive cases on d_real≥0. Hence k_real≥d_real+1 for all cases.\"}, {\"id\": \"S025\", \"text\": \"F004 gives T≥D+1, and N005 gives T≤D+1. Antisymmetry proves T=D+1. Both dimensions and their sum are finite by F001 and N004. When n₀=0, N004 forces D=0 and the fibre branch already supplies the lower bound; the upper proof also covered this case. This proves the output proposition. Compactness remains an original hypothesis in the global specification, although this replacement proof does not use it.\"}, {\"id\": \"S026\", \"text\": \"Fix arbitrary p,z∈L2Prod(n₀). Apply M014 with W=Rn(n₀), Z=ℝ and argument p-z. These are normed additive commutative groups, hence satisfy its seminormed assumptions. With the coordinatewise subtraction and distance as norm of difference, the source gives\\n dist(p,z)²=‖p.fst-z.fst‖²+|p.snd-z.snd|²\\n =Σ_{h<n₀}|p.fst_h-z.fst_h|²+|p.snd-z.snd|².\\nThe second equality uses the defining squared Euclidean norm on Rn(n₀). On the other hand, the defining squared norm on Rn(n₀+1), split into its first n₀ coordinates and last coordinate, gives\\n dist(j(n₀)(p),j(n₀)(z))²=Σ_{h<n₀}|p.fst_h-z.fst_h|²+|p.snd-z.snd|².\\nThe two nonnegative distances have equal squares; hence they are equal. These formulas hold also when n₀=0, with an empty finite sum.\"}, {\"id\": \"S027\", \"text\": \"By F001, j(n₀) is an isometry. Its explicit inverse sends a vector v∈Rn(n₀+1) to the pair consisting of its first n₀ coordinates in Rn(n₀) and its last coordinate, regarded as an element of L2Prod(n₀) using euclideanLift n₀. Coordinate evaluation shows that both composites are the identity. Hence j(n₀) is bijective. By the definitions of F,G,euclideanLift and j, a point of j(n₀) '' (euclideanLift n₀ '' F) is precisely a concatenation of x∈E and t∈I; conversely every such concatenation arises this way. Thus this image equals G. For arbitrary x,y∈Rn(n₀) and real t,u, specialize F001 with p_bound=euclideanLift n₀ (x,t) and z_bound=euclideanLift n₀ (y,u) to obtain the squared-distance formula stated in the output proposition for every such x,y,t,u. For n₀=0 the inverse still extracts the last coordinate and the empty initial tuple; every step is unchanged.\"}, {\"id\": \"S028\", \"text\": \"Apply the exact prepared basis port E001 with its universally bound n equal to the current n₀ and S=F⊆MaxProd(Rn(n₀)). Its identity map on underlying pairs is euclideanLift n₀, with image in L2Prod(n₀). It gives dimH(euclideanLift n₀ '' F)=dimH(F)=T. This is exactly the registered maximum-to-L² product dimension equality.\"}, {\"id\": \"S029\", \"text\": \"N007 proves that j(n₀):L2Prod(n₀)→Rn(n₀+1) is an isometry and that j(n₀) '' (euclideanLift n₀ '' F)=G. Apply M008 with this map and set euclideanLift n₀ '' F. It gives dimH(G)=dimH(euclideanLift n₀ '' F). By F001 and N006 this equals T=D+1=dimH(E)+1. N006 also provides finiteness. By D016, G is exactly the specified Euclidean E×[0,1] under coordinate concatenation, and E on the right retains its original Euclidean Rn(n₀) metric. N007 includes the n₀=0 identification, so there is no excluded ambient dimension. Since the dimensions are finite, the extended nonnegative equality is also equality of the real Hausdorff dimensions. This is the complete selected conclusion.\"}], \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}
{"i":4,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"Mathlib/Analysis/Normed/Lp/PiLp.lean\", \"lines\": \"77: open Module Real Set Filter RCLike Bornology Uniformity Topology NNReal ENNReal WithLp\\n78: \\n79: noncomputable section\\n80: \\n81: /-- A copy of a Pi type, on which we will put the `L^p` distance. Since the Pi type itself is\\n82: already endowed with the `L^∞` distance, we need the type synonym to avoid confusing typeclass\\n83: resolution. Also, we let it depend on `p`, to get a whole family of type on which we can put\\n84: different distances. -/\\n85: abbrev PiLp (p : ℝ≥0∞) {ι : Type*} (α : ι → Type*) : Type _ :=\\n86:   WithLp p (∀ i : ι, α i)\\n87: \\n88: /-The following should not be a `FunLike` instance because then the coercion `⇑` would get\\n89: unfolded to `FunLike.coe` instead of `WithLp.equiv`. -/\\n90: instance (p : ℝ≥0∞) {ι : Type*} (α : ι → Type*) : CoeFun (PiLp p α) (fun _ ↦ (i : ι) → α i) where\"}"}}
{"i":5,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"Mathlib/Analysis/Normed/Lp/WithLp.lean\", \"lines\": \"37: \\n38: TODO: is it safe to copy across the topology and uniform space structure too for all reasonable\\n39: choices of `V`?\\n40: -/\\n41: \\n42: @[expose] public section\\n43: \\n44: \\n45: open scoped ENNReal\\n46: \\n47: /-- A type synonym for the given `V`, associated with the L`p` norm. Note that by default this just\\n48: forgets the norm structure on `V`; it is up to downstream users to implement the L`p` norm (for\\n49: instance, on `Prod` and finite `Pi` types). -/\\n50: structure WithLp (p : ℝ≥0∞) (V : Type*) where\\n51:   /-- Converts an element of `V` to an element of `WithLp p V`. -/\\n52:   toLp (p) ::\\n53:   /-- Converts an element of `WithLp p V` to an element of `V`. -/\\n54:   ofLp : V\\n55: \\n56: section Notation\\n57: \\n58: open Lean.PrettyPrinter.Delaborator\"}"}}
{"i":6,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"path\": \"Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean\", \"lines\": \"53: \\n54: ### Hausdorff measure in `ℝⁿ`\\n55: \\n56: * `Real.dimH_of_nonempty_interior`: if `s` is a set in a finite-dimensional real vector space `E`\\n57:   with nonempty interior, then the Hausdorff dimension of `s` is equal to the dimension of `E`.\\n58: * `dense_compl_of_dimH_lt_finrank`: if `s` is a set in a finite-dimensional real vector space `E`\\n59:   with Hausdorff dimension strictly less than the dimension of `E`, the `s` has a dense complement.\\n60: * `ContDiff.dense_compl_range_of_finrank_lt_finrank`: the complement to the range of a `C¹`\\n61:   smooth map is dense provided that the dimension of the domain is strictly less than the dimension\\n62:   of the codomain.\\n63: \\n64: ## Notation\\n65: \\n66: We use the following notation localized in `MeasureTheory`. It is defined in\\n67: `MeasureTheory.Measure.Hausdorff`.\\n68: \\n69: - `μH[d]` : `MeasureTheory.Measure.hausdorffMeasure d`\\n70: \\n71: ## Implementation notes\\n72: \\n73: * The definition of `dimH` explicitly uses `borel X` as a measurable space structure. This way we\\n74:   can formulate lemmas about Hausdorff dimension without assuming that the environment has a\\n75:   `[MeasurableSpace X]` instance that is equal but possibly not defeq to `borel X`.\\n76: \\n77:   Lemma `dimH_def` unfolds this definition using whatever `[MeasurableSpace X]` instance we have in\\n78:   the environment (as long as it is equal to `borel X`).\\n79: \\n80: * The definition `dimH` is irreducible; use API lemmas or `dimH_def` instead.\\n81: \\n82: ## Tags\\n83: \\n84: Hausdorff measure, Hausdorff dimension, dimension\\n85: -/\\n86: \\n87: @[expose] public section\\n88: \\n89: \\n90: open scoped MeasureTheory ENNReal NNReal Topology\\n91: \\n92: open MeasureTheory MeasureTheory.Measure Set TopologicalSpace Module Filter\\n93: \\n94: variable {ι X Y : Type*} [EMetricSpace X] [EMetricSpace Y]\\n95: \\n96: /-- Hausdorff dimension of a set in an (e)metric space. -/\\n97: @[irreducible] noncomputable def dimH (s : Set X) : ℝ≥0∞ := by\\n98:   borelize X; exact ⨆ (d : ℝ≥0) (_ : @hausdorffMeasure X _ _ ⟨rfl⟩ d s = ∞), d\\n99: \\n100: /-!\\n101: ### Basic properties\\n102: -/\\n103: \\n104: \\n105: section Measurable\\n106: \\n107: variable [MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X]\\n108: \\n109: /-- Unfold the definition of `dimH` using `[MeasurableSpace X] [BorelSpace X]` from the\\n110: environment. -/\\n111: theorem dimH_def (s : Set X) : dimH s = ⨆ (d : ℝ≥0) (_ : μH[d] s = ∞), (d : ℝ≥0∞) := by\\n112:   borelize X; rw [dimH]\\n113: \\n114: theorem hausdorffMeasure_of_lt_dimH {s : Set X} {d : ℝ≥0} (h : ↑d < dimH s) : μH[d] s = ∞ := by\\n115:   simp only [dimH_def, lt_iSup_iff] at h\\n116:   rcases h with ⟨d', hsd', hdd'⟩\\n117:   rw [ENNReal.coe_lt_coe, ← NNReal.coe_lt_coe] at hdd'\\n118:   exact top_unique (hsd' ▸ hausdorffMeasure_mono hdd'.le _)\\n119: \\n120: theorem dimH_le {s : Set X} {d : ℝ≥0∞} (H : ∀ d' : ℝ≥0, μH[d'] s = ∞ → ↑d' ≤ d) : dimH s ≤ d :=\\n121:   (dimH_def s).trans_le <| iSup₂_le H\\n122: \\n123: theorem dimH_le_of_hausdorffMeasure_ne_top {s : Set X} {d : ℝ≥0} (h : μH[d] s ≠ ∞) : dimH s ≤ d :=\\n124:   le_of_not_gt <| mt hausdorffMeasure_of_lt_dimH h\\n125: \\n126: theorem le_dimH_of_hausdorffMeasure_eq_top {s : Set X} {d : ℝ≥0} (h : μH[d] s = ∞) :\\n127:     ↑d ≤ dimH s := by\\n128:   rw [dimH_def]; exact le_iSup₂ (α := ℝ≥0∞) d h\\n129: \\n130: theorem hausdorffMeasure_of_dimH_lt {s : Set X} {d : ℝ≥0} (h : dimH s < d) : μH[d] s = 0 := by\"}"}}

